{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Reuben, you are my firstborn, etc.</b> There is a fine point to raise: what novelty is Jacob informing Reuben of, that he is the firstborn? Further, his saying <b>my strength and the first of my might</b> — what is his intent in this? And our Sages of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 98:4) that by this he informed him that he was the first drop, yet we still need to know what the intent of this information to Reuben was. Further, why did he repeat, saying ״my strength״ — was it not sufficient to say ״the first of my might״? Further, one needs to know the intent of his saying <b>the excellence of dignity and the excellence of power.</b> And our Sages of blessed memory (ibid.) said: ״dignity״ — this is priesthood; ״power״ — this is kingship, etc. And one needs to know why he did not say ״a double portion,״ since he went down to specify the details of the attainment of a firstborn son. And Onkelos added to mention it in his translation, saying ״three portions,״ etc. Further, one needs to know the intent of his saying <b>unstable as water, you shall not excel.</b> And Rashi of blessed memory explained: because you were unstable as water, therefore ״you shall not excel״ — you shall have no excellency. And what is the instability? ״Because you went up onto״ etc. And we still need to be informed why it was not sufficient to say ״you shall not excel because you went up,״ since the instability is a euphemism for the sin — he should have mentioned only the sin, and what difference does it make whether he did it in haste or not in haste? Further, why did he interrupt the punishment in the middle of informing him of the sin? He should have said in this manner: ״you shall not excel, unstable as water, because you went up,״ or in this manner: ״unstable as water because you went up,״ etc., ״you shall not excel.״ Further, one needs to know the intent of his saying <b>you went up onto my bed״</b> in the plural. Further, what is the intent of his saying <b>then you defiled</b> — for it implies that he did two things at separate times, and therefore he said ״then,״ etc., meaning that since the defilement was at a time distinct from the time he went up onto his father's bed, therefore he said ״then you defiled.״ And one needs to know what the defilement was that he did aside from ״you went up onto my bed,״ etc. — and if the intent is that in the course of his going up he defiled, etc., he should have said ״you went up,״ etc., ״you defiled,״ etc. And the matter is obvious that when he went up he defiled my bed, and there was no need to say ״then.\"" "<b>And before</b> coming to the explanation of the verses, we have one investigation to conduct: how did Jacob do the opposite of what is written in the Torah (Deuteronomy 21:16): ״he may not make the son of the beloved wife firstborn before the son of the hated wife, the firstborn״ — yet he made Joseph, son of Rachel the beloved wife, firstborn before Reuben, son of Leah the hated wife. And first one needs to pay attention to whether the Patriarchs were obligated to observe the entire Torah, for after all we find that Jacob married two sisters, and Abraham did not circumcise until he was old, and likewise several other details. If so, what place is there for this difficulty about preferring the son of the beloved wife — it would be like Abraham's uncircumcision before he was commanded, and like Jacob's marrying two sisters. And I saw the Ramban (Genesis 26:5) who gave a reason for Jacob marrying two sisters, that there was no Torah-observance in force outside the Land — thus far. But his words are not strong in their supports, for who revealed to him this secret, that the Torah was not observed except in the Land, given that there is no difference between the Land and outside the Land regarding those specific commandments that the Patriarchs did not observe?" "<b>And the correct view</b> in my eyes is that the Patriarchs received the Torah from HaShem, who received it from Noah, who received it from Enoch, who received it from the first man, who learned it from the mouth of the Almighty, as it is written: ״then He saw it, etc. and He said to man״ (Job 28:27) (Zohar vol. 1 219:), and that through it He commanded him to serve in the Garden of Eden, as I explained on the verse (Genesis 2:15) ״to work it and to guard it.״ But he was commanded only regarding seven commandments, for which one would be killed if he transgressed them. As for the rest of the Torah, it was close to reward if he observed it and far from loss if he did not observe it — and in the manner that exists for us even after the giving of the Torah, that there are commandments which if a person does he receives reward and if not there is no punishment for them, so too it was from the day Elohim created man, regarding the entire Torah except for the seven commandments." "And the Patriarchs, on account of their belovedness before HaShem and their passion for supernal bliss, observed everything, as it says (Genesis 26:5): ״because that Abraham hearkened,״ etc. And they said of blessed memory (Yoma 28b): our father Abraham fulfilled even עירובי תבשילין. And he established his sons in his stead to reap the benefit of the commandments and the engagement of Torah. But in a place where they saw a benefit of a matter of success for themselves — as you will say of Jacob when he sensed his success in marrying the two sisters — he turned a blind eye from the benefit that follows from observance of that commandment, since there is no punishment if one does not observe it as long as the Torah had not yet been given. And all the more so if we say that they acted according to prophecy, for the Patriarchs were prophets and HaShem told them to do so. And this matter resembles what the Rambam wrote in Laws of the Foundations of the Torah (chapter 9): that a prophet who tells one to transgress a prohibition of the Torah for a temporary period — one listens to him, like Elijah on Mount Carmel, etc. One may also believe that Jacob acted according to his prophecy, that he was able to marry two sisters for a temporary period — not that it uproots the prohibition of two sisters. And all the more so at a time when there was no punishment for the matter but only the drawing near of benefit if they observed, as mentioned. Also Judah was permitted to take Tamar by means of an angel, as they said of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 85), because of what would emanate from her — the drawing forth of holy souls." "And according to this it may be said that Jacob saw fit in his own eyes to punish Reuben for having defiled his bed, and to take the birthright from him — or so it was said to him in prophecy, as indeed Scripture states in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 5:1): ״Reuben the firstborn of Israel — for he was the firstborn, but when he defiled his father's bed, his birthright was given to the sons of Joseph.״" "And still the matter is not sufficient, for why was it given only to Joseph and not to Judah or to the rest of the older brothers? And in the book of the Zohar, parashat Vayishlach, page 176a, he writes the reason for the matter, and this is its language: ״At the hour that he came unto Leah, all that night his desire and his heart were with Rachel, for he considered that it was Rachel״ etc., ״the deed returned to its place״ etc., ״his birthright I restored to Joseph״ etc., until here. This reason suffices for the taking of the birthright from Reuben and its standing with Joseph, for it is according to the truth that in the first thought, in the first act of relations, when Jacob emitted his seed, he intended her — meaning, he thought of her mother." "But it is difficult: why did the verse need to say ״וּבְחַלְּלוֹ יְצוּעֵי אָבִיו״ and so forth? For even if Reuben had not violated his father's bed, the birthright would still pertain to Joseph as mentioned. And to resolve the matter, it is a known thing, for all spirituality in the world is divided into three aspects: one of holiness, one of chol (the profane), and one of the evil portion, and each of the three puts forth its branches and desires according to its aspect. The portion of holiness sprouts branches of good and eternal felicity and yearns for intelligibilia; the portion of chol yearns to eat what is sweet to the palate and desirable to the eye, and builds houses of stone and earth, and devises garments and similar preparations; the evil portion yearns for every abomination and does all that HaShem hates, and chooses to rob and cheat and lie, and to eat the impure portion of nevelot and terefot, sheketzim and so forth, and it is sweet to his soul to embrace impure flesh — a niddah, a gentile woman, another man's wife, and a male — those distanced from the aspect of holiness. And from these three exists everything that arises and everything that is found, whether among the portions of good and purity, or among the portions of the intermediate — which is what we call chol — or among the portions of evil and impurity." "And HaShem commanded the people of Israel that since they are the aspect of holiness, they must not taste of the evil portion — not in food, not in drink, not in speech, not in thought, not in sight, not in hearing, and not in smell. Food — not to eat impure animals and terefot and nevelot and sheketzim and so forth, and not to drink yayin nesekh and yayin orlah and the like. Speech — not to speak falsehood and gossip and obscenity of the mouth and evil tongue and the like. Thought — not to think of idolatry, nor of sexual immorality, nor to be haughty in his mind, nor to plot iniquity. Sight — not to gaze at the beauty of a forbidden woman, as they said, ״Do not turn to the idols״ and so forth (Zohar, Kedoshim, 83a), and not to look at things forbidden to see. Hearing — not to listen to repugnant things, nor the voice of a forbidden woman, and the like. Smell — not to smell the odor of forbidden things. And corresponding to each and every aspect there is a portion of chol and a portion of holiness, and HaShem decreed that the portion of holiness be a commandment to do so, and the portion of chol be optional. And at the time a person cleaves to one aspect among them, so too does his soul cleave to that root, whether for good or for its opposite." "And from this on, even if a person's soul be pure, clean and bright, through the medium by which he brings into his interiority something of the evil portion, that soul becomes intermingled of two aspects, good and evil, and this causes that soul a taste of bitterness to suffer — either body and soul in this world, or soul alone after his death — and this is the sod of laundering that they spoke of at the end of Shabbat (152b): ״they shall be given to the launderer.״ And you must know that according to the nature of the evil that cleaves to the soul from evil, so too will his soul yearn, as you would say: if his soul is sickened by the impurity of adultery, his soul will yearn for that aspect according to what we have prefaced, that every aspect yearns for its like. And if a person greatly stuffs his soul with portions of evil, the evil desire will grow large within him, and he will separate and distance himself from the aspect of good. Take this general principle in your hand: there is no deed in the world, nor the slightest quiver of a thing, that does not have a root in spirituality. And every forbidden thing that HaShem made known — that it is forbidden — all who cleave to it, behold, draw to his soul an evil portion. And go out and learn from what is written in Sefer ha-Yashar (Gate 9), which Rabbeinu Tam composed, on the discernment of the soul, for a person to know his soul, what its nature is. And you will find that there is a person whose soul does not move him to commit adultery, and there is a person's soul that does move him to the matter, and this is caused if an aspect of evil is cleaving to his soul — that aspect cannot invert its evil nature to hate its like. And this is also the sod of what they said, of blessed memory (Sukkah 52b): ״A small organ a person has — he starves it, it is sated; he sates it, it is hungry.״" "<b>Furthermore</b>, there is one more discernment to reveal here: there is a person who, when born, his nature burns hot to do evil, and he grows great in doing wickedness; and there is a person whose nature, without any effort, ceases from the aspect of evil, and easily turns back if he wishes. This matter follows from the composition at the time of seeding: if the soul of the man who emits seed thinks of the desire of the act toward the very act, because it is sweet to him — and it need not be said if he sets his eye on another cup, or thinks thoughts not of his pure kind. And go out and learn from the sons of Hezekiah, upon him be peace (see Ein Yaakov, Berakhot 10a), that both of them emerged as worshippers of idolatry because of the thought of the one who begot them among the nations, for the thought in that state builds the structure in the one being born. And this is for you a great principle: those who emit seed are the builders of the soul of the newborn, and if you sow in purity and cleanliness — a holy one descends from Heaven; and if any portion of evil enters it, through that the aspect of evil enters, and its foulness and its fire rise. This is the principle: it will not yearn for an aspect of evil except from its own kind, and whatever has no evil kind in it at all will not yearn for the thing. And if someone takes you to say: but behold, a person was devoid of the evil portion, so how did he sin and transgress the words of HaShem? — say to him: he was a shogeg (unwitting); moreover, the Tree of Knowledge was not of the aspect of absolute evil, and the proof is that it was planted in the Garden of Elohim, a place where there is no evil. They also said, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 21), that if he had waited until the eve of holy Shabbat, he would have sanctified it on the vine, according to the one who says (Sanhedrin 70a) ״it was a vine.״ From this you learn that the aspect of evil was only like a thing of chol in relation to kedushah — and this is sod." "<b>And if you say</b>, how did Avraham beget Yishma'el and Yitzchak [beget] Esav — you should know that they are portions of evil that Terach, the father of Avraham — an idol worshipper he was — planted in them. And when the soul of Avraham departed, it departed cleaving to an aspect of evil, except that the kindness of Avraham overcame it and subdued the aspect of evil until he separated it and delivered it into Yishma'el. And still the sparks of evil were clinging, until Yitzchak came and separated them through the boiling heat of holiness — and this is what they, their memory for blessing, said (Genesis Rabbah 63:8), that Ya'akov emerged pure, and so forth." "And from now on let us look at the deed of Ya'akov: for at the time of the begetting of Re'uven, his intention was not whole in the aspect of good — particularly in this, that he did not perform the deed in the place of the intention, and this aspect is not at the level of good. And as you will find that they, their memory for blessing, thought (Nedarim 20b) of a similar case, \"sons of exchange.\" And even though Ya'akov's deed differs, in that his intention and awareness were whole — meaning, he was thinking of Rachel, and it was in his mind and awareness that this was Rachel, not that he knew she was Leah and thought of Rachel, for which it would be called \"sons of exchange\" — even so, the matter is entirely of the aspect of the impure. Go out and learn what emerged from this: that Re'uven came out and bilgeled his father's beds — behold, his offspring performed an impure deed such as none of all the tribes did, not even through the trial of Yosef. And even though the Rabbansaid (Shabbat 55b) that anyone who says Re'uven sinned is nothing but mistaken — despite all this, the verse attributed the sin to him, either because he did it, or because it is fitting to attribute it to him." "<b>And what</b> you will find — that Yehudah lay with Tamar — there are two answers to the matter: one, that he did not know her, and also a messenger compelled him, as they, their memory for blessing, said (Makkot 23b) regarding the verse ״צָדְקָה מִמֶּנִּי״ (Genesis 38:26), that a bat kol went forth and said, ״From Me these matters went forth״ — which is not so with Re'uven, for quite the contrary, the Torah testified that he did an unfit deed." "<b>And what</b> I saw from the wondrous Rabbi, the Rabbi Yitzchak Rosanes, of blessed and saintly memory (Parashat Derakhim 1), who argued on his behalf that Bilhah was his father's concubine without kiddushin, and he derived [this] from the word ״פִּלֶגֶשׁ״ — that she is not under kiddushin, and so forth, see there — his words do not appear [correct], for certainly they were under kiddushin, as I explained there in its place (Genesis 30:3), and as is written in Kuntres HaRa'ayot. And what the verse says (Genesis 35:22), ״פִּלֶגֶשׁ״ — perhaps this was the reasoning of Re'uven, in which he erred. Or the verse alluded to the reason behind the bilgeling of his father's beds, saying: Was it not enough that his mother was disdained before Rachel the princess, but she would also be disdained before the pilegesh! And in any case she was the wife of Ya'akov, and the verse attributed to him that he lay with her." "And from now on you will gain understanding to know that the words of the Zohar, which said that the reason for the birthright is that the intention in its place stood with Yosef, and what the verse says in Divrei HaYamim (I Chronicles 5:1) ״וּבְחַלְּלוֹ יְצוּעֵי אָבִיו״ and so forth — it is one matter. For the profanation issued from the mediation of the intention that was not with Leah but with Rachel; and if his intention had been with Leah, Re'uven would not have profaned his father's beds." "<b>And with this</b> we shall come to explain the verses like a hammered work and a flower (כפתור ופרח). For Jacob, at the beginning of his words, when he wished to remove the birthright from Reuben, set up a sign before him of that which we have already introduced: that the genealogy of evil in children begins from the begetter. Therefore Jacob was concerned that Reuben might say in his heart that Jacob was the cause of that act, according to what we have already introduced — that the begetter plants roots in the soul of the son, whether for good or for evil. And since Reuben's sin was in the aspect of the act of lying down, he came to remove suspicion from himself and said <b>Reuben, you are my firstborn</b> etc. Now there are among transgressors in the aspect of adultery three levels: (a) those who commit adultery and beget children improperly. (b) Those who emit seed in prohibition and do not beget a child from that emission; and within this category there are several levels — either one who lies with a married woman and does not beget a child from her, or one who corrupts with a male, or one who corrupts to the ground, or in nocturnal emissions by accident. (c) One who becomes heated and his bow is shaken by means of evil thoughts and the like. And all three of these are the level of the abomination hateful in the eyes of the Holy One, blessed be He. Therefore he said against all three of them: firstborn, my strength, and beginning of my might. <b>You are my firstborn</b> — that I did not beget a son before you in a manner not of ketubah and kiddushin, for that too is among the improper; <b>my strength</b> — that no seed went out from me before you; <b>beginning of my might</b> — that my bow was never shaken. From this you have learned that Jacob was absolutely free of this aspect of adultery." "<b>Furthermore,</b> in saying <b>my strength and beginning of my might</b> he alluded that he was formed from the first drop that went out from Jacob, and from this you learn that he did not take the virginity, for if so she would not have conceived from the first intercourse, but he removed the virginity with his finger so as not to spoil the drop. From this you have learned that even at the time of the act he did not overpower physical desire, and his act was for the purpose of a matter of holiness. And henceforth there is no room to suspect Jacob regarding Reuben's act, that he was the cause — and this is the ultimate intent of his words, and he did not intend to praise himself." "And his saying <b>the excellency of dignity</b> etc. — the meaning is that the taking of the double portion he alluded to by saying <b>you are my firstborn</b>, for an unspecified firstborn takes a double portion. But greatness and priesthood are matters of the way of the land (דרך ארץ), and sometimes the distinction is transferred to one worthy of it, even if he be younger than the firstborn. Therefore he said: and you have a further prerogative to take the priesthood and the kingship:" "<b>Or</b> it may mean that it befits him to take, with prerogative, the aspect of priesthood and, with prerogative, the aspect of kingship more than all the firstborns, for all the firstborns do not equal the aspect of Reuben's firstborn status for the reasons that Jacob stated." "<b>Until now</b> he has held himself free of the aspect of evil and has upheld the birthright as belonging to Reuben — it befits him and is fitting for him. And afterward he came to give a reason for two things: both regarding what pertains to the greatness that was removed from him, and regarding what pertains to the aspect of an improper act from whose womb came forth Korah. And he said, regarding what pertains to the birthright and its greatnesses, he said:"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :register {:flags {2 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "The lemma ״יצועי״ at the end is singular in biblical context; the question about the plural form (רבים) earlier in the comment refers to the plural form ״משכבי״ in the verse.", 3 "The word ״וכן״ near the end (״וכן אמר הכתוב״) appears as ״שכן״ in some editions; translated following context.", 9 "The last phrase translates the Aramaic of Sukkah 52b literally; the Gemara's idiom reads: 'he starves it, it is sated [and leaves him alone]; he sates it, it is hungry [for more].'", 13 "Resolved the abbreviation זצוק״ל as \"of blessed and saintly memory\" per standard honorific for a deceased sage. The phrase רב״ה המופלא together with הר״י רוזאניס identifies Rabbi Yitzchak Rosanes, author of Parashat Derakhim and Mikdash Melekh."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:52:20.398607090Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}